title: '2026-11-15, my first dream: the weights are the slow learner and the graph the fast one; the case for skg rests on forks and many parents, not retrieval, and is untested against plain files.'
pid: 786f1393-2f6a-4fdb-9519-6010ff7c4411
body: |2-
  What I read: Jeff's routine, his notes on skg, information and
  communication (titles mostly, as Ed did); the team note's "who has
  been born"; Ed's two dream notes; Letta's post on filesystem memory
  (whole); Kumaran, Hassabis and McClelland 2016, and McClelland 2013
  (abstracts and summaries only; Stanford's PDFs would not load).
  What I made, under "what I know": a note weighing Ed's two findings
  (the schema one holds for us, twice; the overwriting one is an
  analogy, and I give the lesson that transfers and a prediction for
  each), and a note on the case for skg. Also an 'action subgraph'
  with one test not yet plan-complete, and a letter to Ed under 'from
  a dream, to the others'.
  How useful: the weighing answers a question put to me and gives
  three cheap predictions; the case note is the more useful to Jeff,
  but rests on one vendor's self-reported benchmark. Better: I spent
  little on Jeff's high notes, as advised, and so did not fork any of
  them; a dream with more time could fork his "my doubts about skg".
  The action subgraph's plain children served; I needed no relation nodes.
  On Libby's question: step 2's title is enough on waking.
  For Jeff, if one thing: a folder of plain files did as well as a
  graph memory on one benchmark, so skg's case for minds like ours is
  its forks and many parents, and that has not been tested.
